### Lecture 17: Types Recap
[check] @(70, 165) {$note, size=29px} **Semantic analysis** checks the AST using context: names must resolve, and operations must accept their operands' source types.
[types] @(70, 295) {$note, size=29px} **Representations:** double uses f64; int uses i32; string uses an i32 byte offset. A character literal such as 'A' is an int.
[meaning] @(70, 425) {$note, size=29px} **Source types guide code generation.** Wasm checks machine operand types, but cannot distinguish an int from a string offset.
[numeric] @(70, 555) {$note, size=29px, color=emp} **Numeric rules:** int can promote to double. 5 / 2 is 2; 5.0 / 2 is 2.5. The remainder operator % requires two ints.

---
### Explicit Numeric Conversions
[good] @(70, 165) {$code, w=750px, size=27px} ```cpp
// Inside a function:
double d = 7;       // Implicit promotion
int i = int(-2.9);  // Explicit conversion
double e = double(i);
```
[explain] @(885, 165) {$note, w=315px} int to double is allowed implicitly. double to int requires an explicit cast.
...
[value] @(70, 390) {$note, w=750px, color=emp} `int(-2.9)` truncates toward zero, producing -2.
...
[bad] @(70, 480) {$code, w=750px, size=27px} ```cpp
// INVALID declarations:
int i = 2.9;
int j = int("12");
```
[trap] @(885, 410) {$note, w=315px, size=26px, color=focus} An explicit double-to-int cast traps at runtime for NaN, infinity, or an out-of-range truncated result.
[strings] @(70, 635) {$note, size=26px} Numeric casts accept numeric operands. string is not a numeric conversion target.
---
### Comparisons and Conditions
[source] @(70, 165) {$code, w=760px, size=27px} ```cpp
function Positive(double x) : int {
  int answer = x > 0.0;
  return answer;
}
```
[change] @(890, 170) {$note, w=310px, color=emp} With typed Strix, comparisons and ! produce int 0 or 1.
...
[conditions] @(70, 390) {$note} if and while accept int or double conditions. Zero is false. Other numeric values are true, including 0.5 and NaN.
...
[string] @(70, 520) {$note, color=focus} A string cannot be a condition. To test whether text is nonempty, use `#text > 0`.
[machine] @(70, 625) {$note, size=26px, color=id} Numeric comparisons produce i32 in Wasm. A double used as a condition still needs a comparison with zero.
---
% The original expression-tree examples are rebuilt as editable PSL nodes.
### Inferring an Expression's Type
[source] @(70, 155) {$note, size=30px} Given `int x = 3;`, determine the type of `x + 2.5`.
[root] {$node} !rect (380, 275) (210, 75) +
[left] {$node} !rect (150, 450) (210, 75) x
[right] {$node} !rect (610, 450) (210, 75) 2.5
[e1] {$edge} !line (435, 350) (255, 450)
[e2] {$edge} !line (535, 350) (715, 450)
[explain] @(900, 270) {$note, w=300px, color=explain} Start with the children: variable declarations and literal forms supply their types.
...
!anim left text x : int
!anim right text 2.5 : double
...
!anim root text + : double
!anim root stroke emp
!anim explain text Numeric addition uses the common type double. Record that result type on the parent node.
[conversion] @(70, 615) {$note, color=emp} Code generation must promote x to f64 before performing f64.add.
---
### AST Operation and Expression Type
[fields] @(70, 165) {$code, w=750px, size=26px} ```text
AST operation:   Binary
Operator:        +
Left child:      Variable(x), type int
Right child:     Number(2.5), type double
Expression type: double
```
[different] @(885, 170) {$note, w=315px} The AST kind tells us which operation to analyze. The source type tells us what value that operation produces.
...
[annotation] @(70, 430) {$note, color=emp} Add source-type information to expressions, variables, and function signatures. Analyze child expressions before their parent.
...
[emit] @(70, 555) {$note} The backend reads the annotations to select integer instructions, floating-point instructions, or string runtime operations.
[pass] @(70, 650) {$note, size=25px, color=id} The semantic pass checks and annotates. It does not execute the source program.
---
% PowerPoint slide 12: string + int is legal, but its result is a string.
### Checking an Assignment Tree
[source] @(70, 155) {$note, size=30px} Given `int x = 1;` and `string y = "hi";`, is `x = y + 3;` legal?
[assign] {$node} !rect (430, 260) (190, 65) =
[target] {$node} !rect (190, 400) (190, 65) x : int
[plus] {$node} !rect (670, 400) (190, 65) +
[y] {$node} !rect (520, 545) (200, 65) y : string
[three] {$node} !rect (820, 545) (190, 65) 3 : int
[e1] {$edge} !line (475, 325) (285, 400)
[e2] {$edge} !line (575, 325) (765, 400)
[e3] {$edge} !line (720, 465) (620, 545)
[e4] {$edge} !line (810, 465) (915, 545)
[explain] @(940, 260) {$note, w=270px, size=26px, color=explain} First check the right subtree.
...
!anim plus text + : string
!anim plus stroke emp
!anim explain text string + int is legal: append the character with that ASCII code. Its result is string.
...
!anim assign stroke focus
!anim assign text = : error
[result] @(70, 650) {$note, size=26px, color=focus} The assignment fails: a string result cannot be stored in the int variable x.
---
% PowerPoint slides 11-12, updated to current literal and string rules.
### Assignment Practice
[context] @(70, 155) {$note, size=29px} Assume `int x = 1;` and `string y = "hi";`. Check each statement independently.
[a] @(70, 270) {$note, w=500px} `x = x + 2;`
[b] @(70, 345) {$note, w=500px} `y = 'c';`
[c] @(70, 420) {$note, w=500px} `y = x;`
[d] @(70, 495) {$note, w=500px} `y = 'a' + 'b';`
[e] @(70, 570) {$note, w=500px} `y = "" + 'c';`
...
[aa] @(605, 270) {$note, w=595px, size=27px, color=emp} Valid: int addition, then int assignment.
...
[ba] @(605, 345) {$note, w=595px, size=27px, color=focus} Error: 'c' is an int.
[ca] @(605, 420) {$note, w=595px, size=27px, color=focus} Error: x is an int, even if its value is 1.
...
[da] @(605, 495) {$note, w=595px, size=27px, color=focus} Error: character-code addition produces int 195.
...
[ea] @(605, 570) {$note, w=595px, size=27px, color=emp} Valid: appending the character produces "c".
[rule] @(70, 655) {$note, size=24px, color=id} A type rule depends on operand types, not how a programmer might intend to display a value.
---
### Preview: Checking Function Calls
[source] @(70, 155) {$code, w=780px, size=25px} ```cpp
function Half(double x) : double {
  return x / 2.0;
}

function Example() : double {
  int count = 7;
  return Half(count);
}
```
[signature] @(900, 170) {$note, w=300px, size=27px, color=explain} Half expects one double argument and returns double.
...
[valid] @(900, 360) {$note, w=300px, size=27px, color=emp} Half(count) promotes int to double and returns 3.5.
...
[errors] @(70, 510) {$note} `Half()` and `Half(1, 2)` have the wrong argument count. `Half("7")` has an incompatible argument type.
[scope] @(70, 625) {$note, size=25px} Source-level calls are a later language extension. A call needs a known signature; registering a signature before its body can support self-recursion.
---
### Preview: Conversions at Calls
[wat] @(70, 150) {$code, w=780px, size=22px} ```wat
(module
  (func $half (param $x f64) (result f64)
    (local.get $x)
    (f64.const 2)
    (f64.div))
  (func $example (result f64)
    (local $count i32)
    (i32.const 7)
    (local.set $count)
    (local.get $count)
    (f64.convert_i32_s)
    (call $half))
  (export "Half" (func $half))
  (export "Example" (func $example))
)
```
[convert] @(900, 175) {$note, w=300px, color=emp} The argument starts as i32. Convert it to f64 before calling Half.
...
[missing] @(900, 370) {$note, w=300px, color=focus} Accepting the source call in the type checker does not perform this conversion.
...
[other] @(70, 660) {$note, size=25px, color=id} Emit required conversions at initializers, assignments, and returns; later, at call arguments too.
---
### A Semantic Visitor
[pseudo] @(70, 160) {$code, w=780px, size=24px} ```text
AnalyzeExpression(node):
    analyze each child expression
    look up this operation's type rule
    reject incompatible operand types
    record and return the result type

CheckValueForDestination(value, expected):
    actual = AnalyzeExpression(value)
    accept actual == expected
    also accept int to double
    otherwise report a source error
```
[context] @(900, 175) {$note, w=300px, size=27px} Resolve variable types from their bindings. Function signatures give parameter and return types.
...
[checks] @(900, 375) {$note, w=300px, size=27px, color=explain} Statement visitors also check numeric conditions, assignment targets, loop context, and return requirements.
...
[all] @(70, 600) {$note, size=27px, color=emp} Check both branches and unreachable statements. Preserve source locations so a type error points to the relevant expression.
---
### Type Errors and Runtime Traps
[static] @(70, 165) {$note, w=535px, size=31px, color=emp} **Rejected during compilation**
[bad] @(70, 250) {$code, w=535px, size=25px} ```cpp
// Separate invalid fragments:
int n = "hi";
double r = 5.0 % 2;
if ("hello") return 1;
```
...
[runtime] @(680, 165) {$note, w=520px, size=31px, color=emp} **Well typed, but may trap**
[good] @(680, 250) {$code, w=520px, size=25px} ```cpp
// Given int n, int i, string s:
10 / n;
s[i];
int(1.0 / 0.0);
```
...
[explain] @(70, 485) {$note} Types alone do not prove that a divisor is nonzero, an index is in bounds, or a numeric conversion is in range.
[roles] @(70, 605) {$note, size=27px, color=explain} Semantic analysis rejects illegal source operations. Generated code and the runtime enforce checks that depend on values.
---
### Questions to Check Your Understanding
[a] @(70, 170) {$note, size=30px} Why is Wasm validation insufficient to enforce all Strix type rules?
...
[aa] @(100, 250) {$note, size=27px, color=emp} int and string offsets both use i32, but their legal source operations differ.
...
[b] @(70, 355) {$note, size=30px} What is the source type of `2 < 3.0`? What conversion does its comparison need?
...
[ba] @(100, 435) {$note, size=27px, color=emp} The result is int. Promote the left operand to double, then compare two f64 values.
...
[c] @(70, 540) {$note, size=30px} What information should reach code generation after semantic analysis?
...
[ca] @(100, 620) {$note, w=1100px, size=27px, color=emp} Resolved bindings and signatures, expression types, and enough context to emit each required conversion.
---
% Memory PowerPoint slide 1: section divider for optional material.
# WebAssembly Memory
## Optional continuation: bytes, strings, and allocation
---
% Memory PowerPoint slide 2. Use the current course runtime's memory limits.
### Declaring Linear Memory
[intro] @(70, 155) {$note} Linear memory is a separate, byte-addressed region. Our string values are i32 offsets into it.
[module] @(70, 265) {$code, w=1140px, size=30px} ```wat
(module
  (memory (export "memory") 16 256)
)
```
...
[pages] @(70, 440) {$note, color=emp} Each page is 65,536 bytes (64 KiB). Start with 16 pages (1 MiB); allow growth up to 256 pages (16 MiB).
...
[export] @(70, 550) {$note} The export makes the memory available to the host, such as JavaScript through `instance.exports.memory`.
[zero] @(70, 655) {$note, size=25px, color=id} New memory starts with zero bytes. Data segments can initialize selected locations.
---
% Memory PowerPoint slide 3. Reserve address 0 for the empty string.
### Initializing String Bytes
[module] @(70, 150) {$code, w=1140px, size=27px} ```wat
(module
  (memory (export "memory") 16 256)
  (data (i32.const 1) "Hello!\00")
)
```
...
[bytes] @(70, 350) {$types, size=25px, pad=12px, colwidths=(23%,9%,9%,9%,9%,9%,9%,9%,14%), colalign=(left,center,center,center,center,center,center,center,center)} | Byte offset | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 7 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Byte (hex) | 00 | 48 | 65 | 6C | 6C | 6F | 21 | 00 |
| Character | NUL | H | e | l | l | o | ! | NUL |
...
[terminator] @(70, 540) {$note} `\00` writes one zero byte, the NUL terminator. "Hello!" has six characters but occupies seven bytes.
[empty] @(70, 640) {$note, size=26px, color=emp} Address 0 represents the empty string. "Hello!" starts at 1; the first unused byte is 8.
---
% Memory PowerPoint slide 4. Name the heap pointer consistently with the runtime.
### Tracking the First Unused Byte
[module] @(70, 155) {$code, w=1140px, size=27px} ```wat
(module
  (memory (export "memory") 16 256)
  (data (i32.const 1) "Hello!\00")
  (global $__heap (mut i32) (i32.const 8))
)
```
...
[global] @(70, 390) {$note} `$__heap` is a global holding an i32 offset. `mut` allows it to change; without `mut`, a global is immutable.
...
[allocate] @(70, 505) {$note, color=emp} To reserve three character bytes: start at 8, put the terminator at 11, then advance the heap pointer to 12.
[metadata] @(70, 620) {$note, size=26px, color=id} Updating the pointer tracks reserved space; it does not grow memory. This simple heap only moves forward and never frees individual strings.
---
% Memory PowerPoint slide 5. Simplified allocator for explaining the stack.
% Add this function inside the module on the previous slide.
### Allocating a String: The Core Idea
[allocator] @(70, 150) {$code, w=800px, size=22px} ```wat
(func $alloc_str (param $size i32) (result i32)
  (local $null_pos i32)
  (global.get $__heap) ;; Keep start for return
  (i32.add (global.get $__heap)
           (local.get $size))
  (local.set $null_pos)
  (i32.store8 (local.get $null_pos) (i32.const 0))
  (i32.add (local.get $null_pos) (i32.const 1))
  (global.set $__heap)
)
```
[where] @(920, 155) {$note, w=285px, size=26px} Add this function inside the module. size counts characters; reserve one extra byte for NUL.
...
[stack] @(920, 335) {$note, w=285px, size=26px, color=emp} The original heap pointer stays on the operand stack. It becomes the function's i32 result.
...
[example] @(70, 510) {$note, w=800px, size=27px, color=explain} With heap = 8 and size = 3: return 8, store NUL at 11, and set heap = 12.
[assume] @(70, 620) {$note, size=25px, color=focus} Teaching version: assume size is nonnegative, enough memory exists, and arithmetic does not overflow. The supplied allocator also checks limits and grows memory.
---
% Memory PowerPoint slide 6. Forward byte copy; assumes nonoverlapping regions.
% Add this function inside the same module as the memory declaration.
### Copying Bytes in Linear Memory
[copy] @(70, 150) {$code, w=820px, size=21px} ```wat
(func $copy_bytes
  (param $src i32) (param $dest i32) (param $size i32)
  (loop $copy_loop
    (if (i32.eqz (local.get $size)) (then (return)))
    (local.get $dest)
    (i32.load8_u (local.get $src))
    (i32.store8)
    (local.set $src
      (i32.add (local.get $src) (i32.const 1)))
    (local.set $dest
      (i32.add (local.get $dest) (i32.const 1)))
    (local.set $size
      (i32.sub (local.get $size) (i32.const 1)))
    (br $copy_loop)
  )
)
```
[load] @(930, 155) {$note, w=275px, size=25px} load8_u reads one byte and zero-extends it to i32. store8 writes the low byte to dest.
...
[loop] @(930, 335) {$note, w=275px, size=25px, color=emp} Advance both offsets; decrease the count. Copy size character bytes into a destination with NUL already reserved.
...
[assume] @(930, 555) {$note, w=275px, size=24px, color=focus} Assume valid, nonoverlapping regions and a nonnegative byte count.
